%I A398417 #9 Aug 03 2026 01:34:30
%S A398417 3,3,5,6,3,2,3,3,3,3
%N A398417 Minimum number of cycles whose length is a power of 2 over all connected cubic graphs on 2n vertices.
%C A398417 A cycle whose length is a power of 2 (i.e., 4, 8, 16, ...) is called a 2-power cycle. On at most 31 vertices the only possible 2-power cycle lengths are 4, 8 and 16.
%C A398417 The Erdős-Gyárfás conjecture (1995) asserts that every graph with minimum degree at least 3 contains a 2-power cycle; restricted to cubic graphs it states precisely that a(n) >= 1 for all n. Any counterexample must have at least 32 vertices.
%C A398417 a(n) = 1 would mean that a cubic graph with a unique 2-power cycle exists on 2n vertices ("2-power unicyclic"); the data shows that no such graph exists on at most 22 vertices. Whether one exists at all is an open question.
%H A398417 Jaan Allikvere, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21650784">2-Power Unicyclic Cubic Graphs Revisited: A Correction and the Minimum Number of Power-of-Two Cycles</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%e A398417 a(7) = 2 is attained by exactly 3 graphs, each containing two 4-cycles and no other 2-power cycle.
%Y A398417 Cf. A002851.
%K A398417 nonn,hard,more
%O A398417 2,1
